(function () {
  "use strict";
  const C = window.JSCourse;
  const M = C.addModule;
  const code = C.code;

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"amber", icon:"mechanics", title:"Object mechanics, protocols and controlled metaprogramming", minutes:165, prerequisites:["JS-021"],
    summary:"Go beneath class syntax to JavaScript's prototype-based object model, property descriptors and built-in protocols. Use iterators, generators, symbols, Proxy and Reflect only when their runtime costs and surprising behavior are justified.",
    outcomes:["Trace property lookup through a prototype chain","Choose composition, classes or delegation from the behavior required","Implement iterable and async-iterable protocols","Use descriptors, Proxy and Reflect without breaking object invariants"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"Objects delegate through prototypes",text:["Every ordinary object has an internal prototype reference. When an own property is absent, lookup follows that reference until it finds a property or reaches null. A class declaration gives familiar constructor and method syntax, but its instance methods still live on the constructor's prototype and are shared by instances.","Own-property checks and prototype lookup answer different questions. Use `Object.hasOwn(value, key)` when untrusted or dictionary-like data must supply the property itself. For pure string-key dictionaries, `Object.create(null)` avoids inherited names, although `Map` is usually clearer when keys are dynamic or non-string."]},
      {title:"Identity, private state and composition are design choices",text:["A class is useful when instances share behavior, construction protects invariants and identity matters. Private fields enforce privacy at runtime and cannot be read through bracket notation. They do not make an object immutable, nor do they validate external data before construction.","Composition assembles focused capabilities without a deep inheritance hierarchy. Prefer it when behaviors vary independently or when a subclass would need to override assumptions of its parent. Inheritance is strongest for a genuine substitutable relationship with a small stable contract, not merely to reuse two methods."]},
      {title:"Protocols let values participate in language features",text:["An iterable supplies a function at `Symbol.iterator` that returns an iterator. The iterator's `next()` method returns `{ value, done }`. Arrays, strings, maps, sets, spread, `for...of` and many APIs meet at this protocol rather than at a shared collection superclass.","A generator function builds the iterator state machine for you and pauses at each `yield`. Async iterables use `Symbol.asyncIterator`; `for await...of` waits for each result and should be paired with cancellation or cleanup when the producer owns files, sockets or other resources."]},
      {title:"Descriptors and proxies change fundamental behavior",text:["A property descriptor controls writability, enumerability and configurability or defines getter/setter access. Defaults differ between ordinary assignment and `Object.defineProperty`, so inspect descriptors when behavior appears invisible to loops or refuses reassignment.","Proxy can intercept operations such as reading, writing and key enumeration; Reflect performs the corresponding default operation. A proxy must obey language invariants and can obscure control flow, object identity and performance. Prefer an explicit function or class unless transparent interception is the actual requirement."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"Property read",steps:[{name:"Receiver",detail:"the object being read"},{name:"Own descriptor",detail:"data value or getter"},{name:"Prototype",detail:"delegate when absent"},{name:"Chain",detail:"repeat lookup"},{name:"Result",detail:"value or undefined"}]},
    example:{title:"A restartable iterable range",language:"javascript",source:code(["export class Range {","  #start; #end;","  constructor(start, end) {","    if (!Number.isInteger(start) || !Number.isInteger(end) || end < start) {","      throw new RangeError(\"Expected integer start <= end\");","    }","    this.#start = start;","    this.#end = end;","  }","","  *[Symbol.iterator]() {","    for (let value = this.#start; value <= this.#end; value += 1) {","      yield value;","    }","  }","}","","console.log([...new Range(2, 5)]); // [2, 3, 4, 5]"]),notes:["Construction rejects states the class cannot represent.","Each iteration creates a fresh generator, so the range is restartable.","Spread works because it consumes the iterable protocol, not because Range extends Array."]},
    practice:{title:"Build a paginated async iterable",brief:"Wrap a page-fetching function in an async iterable that yields records, stops on the final cursor and releases obsolete work when the caller cancels.",steps:["Define the page contract and keep remote data unknown until validated.","Implement `Symbol.asyncIterator` with an async generator.","Pass an AbortSignal into every page request.","Prevent a repeated cursor from creating an infinite loop.","Use `try/finally` for cleanup and test early loop termination.","Document retry ownership so pages are not duplicated silently."],starter:code(["export function paginated(fetchPage, { signal } = {}) {","  return {","    async *[Symbol.asyncIterator]() {","      let cursor;","      const seen = new Set();","      // fetch, validate, yield, and advance until no cursor remains","    }","  };","}"]),criteria:["Iteration validates every remote page","A repeated cursor fails with a diagnostic error","Abort stops the active request","Breaking from the loop performs cleanup","Tests cover empty, one-page, multi-page, malformed and cancelled flows"],hints:["An async generator's `finally` block runs when the consumer closes it.","Track cursors before fetching the next page, not after an unbounded loop."]},
    diagnostic:{title:"Method lost its receiver",prompt:"`const run = service.run; run()` throws because `this` is undefined. What happened?",options:["The method was detached from its receiver","The prototype was deleted","Generators require await","Private fields became public"],answer:0,explanation:"A method call supplies its receiver through the call expression. Extracting the function removes that receiver unless the API binds or avoids `this`."},
    checks:[{q:"Where are class instance methods normally stored?",options:["Copied into every instance","On the constructor's prototype","In localStorage","Inside the event loop"],answer:1,why:"Class syntax installs shared instance methods on the prototype."},{q:"What enables spread and `for...of` for a custom value?",options:["A length property","The iterable protocol at Symbol.iterator","Extending Array only","A Proxy get trap"],answer:1,why:"These language features consume the iterable protocol."},{q:"When is Proxy the best default?",options:["For all validation","Only when operation interception is truly required and invariants are understood","To make objects immutable","To replace modules"],answer:1,why:"Explicit APIs are easier to reason about unless transparent interception is the requirement."}],
    project:"Create a small collection library with an immutable validated value object, a restartable iterable, a cancellable async iterable and descriptor-level tests; justify every use of inheritance, symbols or interception.",
    resources:["ecma262","mdn-reference","eloquent","ydkjs"]
  });

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"blue", icon:"types", title:"TypeScript as a design and migration tool", minutes:155, prerequisites:["JS-021"],
    summary:"TypeScript checks a model of your JavaScript before execution; it does not validate network data or change runtime semantics. Learn inference, unions, narrowing, generics, module configuration, strictness and an incremental migration strategy grounded in real boundaries.",
    outcomes:["Explain what TypeScript proves and what it cannot prove","Model domain states with unions and narrowing","Use generics to preserve relationships rather than hide unknowns","Migrate JavaScript incrementally under strict checks"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"Static types disappear before runtime",text:["TypeScript analyzes source, reports mismatches and emits JavaScript. Type assertions such as `as User` do not parse or validate a payload; they tell the checker to trust the programmer. External JSON, environment variables and database rows still require runtime validation before being treated as trusted domain values.","Inference is usually strongest near local values. Add annotations at public boundaries, recursive definitions or places where inference would widen intent. Avoid annotating every obvious literal because redundant types become maintenance noise."]},
      {title:"Model valid states instead of optional-property soup",text:["A discriminated union gives each state a stable tag and only the fields valid in that state. Narrow with `switch`, property checks or predicates. An exhaustive `never` check turns a newly added variant into compile-time work at every decision point.","Optional properties are appropriate when absence is independently valid. They are a poor substitute for mutually exclusive loading, success and failure states because they allow impossible combinations such as data and error simultaneously."]},
      {title:"Generics preserve a relationship",text:["A generic is useful when output type relates to input type or several values share a type parameter. Constraints state required capabilities. A generic named `T` that is immediately cast to `any` preserves no information and only decorates uncertainty.","Use `unknown` for values whose type is not established. Code must narrow it before use. `any` disables checking and spreads through expressions, so confine it to a documented migration edge when no safer choice exists."]},
      {title:"Configuration is part of correctness",text:["Enable strict checking for new code. `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, exact optional-property behavior and other stricter flags reveal assumptions but may require deliberate migration. Align `module` and `moduleResolution` with the actual Node or bundler environment; a configuration that type-checks imports Node cannot run is not success.","Migrate by boundary: enable `checkJs` and JSDoc where useful, convert stable leaf modules, define runtime decoders at external edges and move inward. Do not combine a language migration with unrelated architecture changes unless separately authorized and evidenced."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"Trust boundary",steps:[{name:"unknown input",detail:"JSON, env or row"},{name:"runtime parser",detail:"validate shape and rules"},{name:"domain type",detail:"trusted representation"},{name:"TypeScript",detail:"preserve relationships"},{name:"JavaScript output",detail:"runtime behavior"}]},
    example:{title:"Discriminated result with exhaustive handling",language:"typescript",source:code(["type LoadResult<T> =","  | { state: \"loading\" }","  | { state: \"success\"; data: T }","  | { state: \"failure\"; message: string };","","function describe<T>(result: LoadResult<T>): string {","  switch (result.state) {","    case \"loading\": return \"Loading…\";","    case \"success\": return \"Ready\";","    case \"failure\": return result.message;","    default: {","      const unreachable: never = result;","      return unreachable;","    }","  }","}"]),notes:["Each variant exposes only valid fields.","Switching on `state` narrows the union.","The `never` assignment detects unhandled future variants."]},
    practice:{title:"Migrate an API boundary without lying",brief:"Convert a JavaScript course loader to TypeScript while keeping the fetched JSON unknown until runtime validation succeeds.",steps:["Define the trusted Course domain type.","Keep `response.json()` as unknown.","Write or adopt a runtime parser with useful field errors.","Return `Promise<Course>` only after parsing.","Enable strict checks and add failure tests for missing, extra and wrong-type fields."],starter:code(["type Course = { id: string; title: string; modules: string[] };","","export async function loadCourse(id: string): Promise<Course> {","  const response = await fetch(`/api/courses/${encodeURIComponent(id)}`);","  const value: unknown = await response.json();","  // validate value before returning Course","}"]),criteria:["No `as Course` assertion replaces validation","Malformed remote data produces a controlled boundary error","The emitted JavaScript runs under the selected module configuration","Strict type checking and runtime tests both pass"],hints:["A type guard must check every field the domain later relies on.","Static and runtime verification are complementary, not interchangeable."]},
    diagnostic:{title:"Compiled successfully, crashed on payload",prompt:"The code used `(await response.json()) as User` and later read `user.name.toUpperCase()`. Why did TypeScript not prevent the crash?",options:["Assertions do not validate runtime data","Strings are not typed","Fetch disables TypeScript","The browser ignored the compiler"],answer:0,explanation:"The assertion overrode the checker's uncertainty; the runtime received no validator."},
    checks:[{q:"What should external JSON initially be typed as?",options:["any","unknown","never","void"],answer:1,why:"Unknown requires runtime proof before the value can be used as trusted domain data."},{q:"What is a strong use of generics?",options:["Replacing every type with T","Preserving the relationship between input and output types","Avoiding runtime validation","Making errors disappear"],answer:1,why:"Generics express reusable relationships while retaining information."},{q:"Does successful type checking prove runtime compatibility?",options:["Always","No; emitted modules and actual host configuration must also be executed","Only for arrays","Only in VS Code"],answer:1,why:"Type checking can succeed for configuration that the runtime cannot load."}],
    project:"Migrate the intermediate service one owned boundary at a time, adding runtime decoders, strict configuration, type tests where useful and a ledger separating compile-time from runtime evidence.",
    resources:["typescript","node-api","vite"]
  });

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"cyan", icon:"react", title:"React and Vite with intentional state and effects", minutes:165, prerequisites:["JS-022"],
    summary:"React renders interfaces from state; Vite supplies a development and build pipeline. Learn component boundaries, state ownership, derived data, effects, async races, accessibility and the difference between a successful build and verified user behavior.",
    outcomes:["Design components around responsibilities and data flow","Keep minimal state and derive the rest","Use effects only to synchronize with external systems","Build accessible loading, error, empty and success states"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"Rendering is a calculation from props and state",text:["A component should be pure during rendering: given the same props and state, it describes the same interface. Do not start fetches, write storage or mutate external objects during render. React may render more than once while reconciling or under development checks.","Keep state at the lowest common owner that needs to coordinate it. Pass data down and events up. Context can avoid repetitive passing for genuinely cross-cutting data, but global context for every field causes broad coupling and rerenders."]},
      {title:"Store sources of truth, derive everything else",text:["If filtered modules can be calculated from modules and a query, do not store a second filtered array that can become stale. If state changes depend on prior state, use the functional updater form. Treat arrays and objects as immutable values for updates so React can observe changed identity.","Reducers help when several events transition related state and impossible combinations should be avoided. They are not mandatory for every toggle; choose the simplest structure that preserves clear state transitions."]},
      {title:"Effects synchronize external systems",text:["An effect connects React to something outside rendering: network requests, browser APIs, subscriptions or imperative widgets. The dependency list describes which reactive values the synchronization uses. Removing a dependency to silence a lint warning usually hides stale behavior.","Cleanup cancels or reverses the previous synchronization. For requests, use AbortController or an identity guard so an older response cannot replace newer state. Prefer event handlers for work caused by a user event and derived values for calculations; neither needs an effect."]},
      {title:"Vite builds assets, not confidence",text:["Vite provides a dev server, module transformation and production build. Environment variables exposed to client code become public bundle content; never put server secrets in a `VITE_` variable. Base paths and static asset URLs must match the intended host.","A successful production build proves asset generation. It does not prove routing on GitHub Pages, network contracts, accessibility, browser support or target-device behavior. Preview the build and run representative browser journeys."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"React data flow",steps:[{name:"Props",detail:"owned data enters"},{name:"Render",detail:"pure UI calculation"},{name:"User event",detail:"explicit intent"},{name:"State update",detail:"new source of truth"},{name:"Effect",detail:"external synchronization only"},{name:"Cleanup",detail:"cancel prior ownership"}]},
    example:{title:"Cancellable data effect",language:"tsx",source:code(["function CourseView({ courseId }: { courseId: string }) {","  const [result, setResult] = useState<LoadResult<Course>>({ state: \"loading\" });","","  useEffect(() => {","    const controller = new AbortController();","    setResult({ state: \"loading\" });","    loadCourse(courseId, controller.signal)","      .then(data => setResult({ state: \"success\", data }))","      .catch(error => {","        if (error.name !== \"AbortError\") {","          setResult({ state: \"failure\", message: \"Course unavailable\" });","        }","      });","    return () => controller.abort();","  }, [courseId]);","","  return renderResult(result);","}"]),notes:["Changing courseId aborts the previous request.","The union prevents simultaneous loading, data and error states.","The UI receives a safe message; detailed causes belong in operational evidence."]},
    practice:{title:"Build the progress dashboard",brief:"Create a Vite React TypeScript interface for resume, completion and progress while keeping the existing server authoritative.",steps:["Create semantic components for navigation, resume card and progress list.","Define one typed API client that runtime-validates responses.","Model request state as a discriminated union.","Implement cancellation and stale-response protection.","Test keyboard flow, accessible names, empty/error states and a production preview.","Record the configured base path and hosting assumptions."],starter:code(["type ProgressState =","  | { state: \"loading\" }","  | { state: \"empty\" }","  | { state: \"ready\"; modules: ModuleProgress[] }","  | { state: \"failure\"; message: string };"]),criteria:["No server secret appears in client environment or bundle","Every async state has usable UI","Keyboard focus remains logical after updates","A stale response cannot replace newer state","Production build and preview both run","Server authorization tests remain independent of UI tests"],hints:["The client base URL is configuration, but credentials remain server-side.","Use browser E2E for behavior and component/unit tests for state transitions."]},
    diagnostic:{title:"Old course data replaced the new course",prompt:"Two fetches overlapped and the slower first response updated state last. What control is missing?",options:["Request cancellation or response identity","A CSS reset","A class constructor","A database index"],answer:0,explanation:"The component must cancel obsolete work or ignore results that no longer match current intent."},
    checks:[{q:"What belongs in React state?",options:["Every value visible in render","The minimal source of truth that changes over time","All filtered copies","Server secrets"],answer:1,why:"Derived values should be calculated to avoid synchronization defects."},{q:"When is an effect appropriate?",options:["For every calculation","To synchronize with an external system","To format a label","To handle a button click that can run directly"],answer:1,why:"Effects are escape hatches for external synchronization."},{q:"What does `vite build` prove?",options:["The app is accessible and deployed","Production assets were generated successfully","The API authorizes correctly","The device experience is verified"],answer:1,why:"Behavioral and deployment claims require separate evidence."}],
    project:"Deliver a typed accessible progress dashboard, API decoder, cancellation-safe data flow, component and browser tests, production preview and a build/deployment evidence boundary.",
    resources:["react","vite","typescript","web-accessibility"]
  });

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"red", icon:"shield", title:"Authentication, authorization and application security", minutes:180, prerequisites:["JS-021"],
    summary:"Security is a system of boundaries and failure assumptions, not one middleware package. Threat-model the learning platform, design sessions, enforce object-level authorization, validate input, protect secrets and dependencies, and test negative paths directly.",
    outcomes:["Distinguish authentication, session and authorization decisions","Model threats and trust boundaries","Design secure cookie-based session behavior","Test object-level access, input abuse and sensitive-data handling"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"Threat modeling begins with assets and boundaries",text:["Identify assets such as credentials, sessions, learner records and instructor content; actors such as anonymous users, learners, instructors and operators; entry points such as browser, API and administration; and trust boundaries between them. Describe credible abuse cases and prioritize by likelihood and impact.","Controls follow threats. A generic list of headers does not address cross-user record access; an authorization policy and negative tests do. Revisit the model when architecture or data changes."]},
      {title:"Authentication establishes a principal",text:["Passwords require a dedicated password-hashing algorithm with appropriate parameters, rate limiting and a recovery design. Multi-factor authentication and reauthentication depend on risk and product requirements. Do not invent cryptographic protocols.","A session identifier should be unpredictable, rotated when privilege changes and invalidated on logout or server-side expiry. Cookie sessions should use Secure, HttpOnly and an appropriate SameSite policy. If state-changing cross-site requests remain possible, add a CSRF defense compatible with the architecture."]},
      {title:"Authorization is checked for every action and object",text:["Roles can grant broad capabilities, but object ownership and relationships still need policy. `GET /learners/alice` must not trust that a logged-in user named Bob chose Alice's URL accidentally. Load the target, evaluate the authenticated principal against the required action and return a response that does not reveal unnecessary existence details.","Central helpers improve consistency, but policy should remain visible and testable in the service boundary. Deny by default when no rule grants access. UI visibility is never the enforcement point."]},
      {title:"Security includes limits, secrets, output and supply chain",text:["Validate types, lengths, counts and formats; constrain body size, query complexity, upload type and rate. Encode output for its context and use parameterized SQL. Keep secrets outside source and client bundles, rotate them and minimize which component can read them.","Pin and review dependencies, inspect lockfile changes, remove unused packages and respond to advisories with context. Log security-relevant events without storing passwords, raw tokens or sensitive bodies. Error responses stay safe while internal records retain a correlation ID and cause."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"Authorized request",steps:[{name:"Credential",detail:"cookie or token arrives"},{name:"Authenticate",detail:"establish principal"},{name:"Load target",detail:"trusted server data"},{name:"Authorize",detail:"principal + action + object"},{name:"Operate",detail:"validated bounded change"},{name:"Audit",detail:"safe event and correlation"}]},
    example:{title:"Object-level policy at the service",language:"typescript",source:code(["async function readProgress(principal: Principal, learnerId: string) {","  const learner = await learners.findById(learnerId);","  if (!learner) throw new NotFoundError();","","  const mayRead =","    principal.role === \"instructor\" ||","    principal.learnerId === learner.id;","","  if (!mayRead) throw new ForbiddenError();","  return progress.forLearner(learner.id);","}"]),notes:["The client-provided learnerId identifies the target, not the caller.","The server loads trusted object context before policy.","Negative cross-user tests must exercise this branch."]},
    practice:{title:"Threat-model and test the progress service",brief:"Produce a threat table, implement the three highest-value controls and add direct negative tests.",steps:["Draw assets, actors, entry points and trust boundaries.","List abuse cases including credential attack, cross-user access, injection, oversized body and token theft.","Rank by likelihood and impact with assumptions.","Implement object policy, body limits and safe session cookie configuration.","Test anonymous, expired-session, wrong-user and wrong-role paths.","Review logs and errors for sensitive leakage."],starter:code(["Threat | Asset | Boundary | Abuse | Control | Direct test","Cross-user read | progress | API/service | change learnerId | object policy | Bob requests Alice","Oversized body | availability | HTTP parser | memory pressure | 32kb limit | 33kb request"]),criteria:["Controls map to named threats","Authorization is tested with a different authenticated user","Secrets and session values never appear in client bundles or logs","Input limits fail safely","The report states residual risks and unverified external controls"],hints:["Start with assets and actions, not a generic checklist.","Use the same route with different authenticated principals to prove object policy."]},
    diagnostic:{title:"The route required login but leaked another learner",prompt:"What control was missing?",options:["Authentication","Object-level authorization","Syntax highlighting","A foreign key"],answer:1,explanation:"Authentication identified the caller; authorization still had to evaluate that caller against the requested learner object."},
    checks:[{q:"What does HttpOnly protect?",options:["It prevents JavaScript from reading the cookie","It encrypts the database","It authorizes objects","It validates JSON"],answer:0,why:"HttpOnly reduces token exposure to script but does not prevent all session attacks."},{q:"What should be the default authorization decision?",options:["Allow unless blocked","Deny unless a rule grants the action","Ask the browser","Use CORS"],answer:1,why:"Deny-by-default prevents unmodeled access from becoming permitted."},{q:"Why keep correlation IDs?",options:["To expose stack traces to clients","To connect a safe response with internal diagnostic evidence","To replace authentication","To format logs as HTML"],answer:1,why:"Operators can investigate without leaking implementation details."}],
    project:"Deliver a threat model and hardened session/authorization slice with negative integration tests, safe logs, dependency review and a residual-risk statement.",
    resources:["owasp-auth","owasp-api","owasp-node","express"]
  });

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"violet", icon:"architecture", title:"Architecture, contracts and evolvable boundaries", minutes:165, prerequisites:["JS-023"],
    summary:"Architecture is the set of decisions that shape change, failure and ownership. Design a modular monolith first, document context and runtime views, make contracts explicit and evolve APIs and data without unnecessary distributed complexity.",
    outcomes:["Use architecture views to answer different questions","Define module ownership and dependency direction","Evolve HTTP and event contracts compatibly","Choose modular monolith versus services from constraints"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"One diagram cannot explain every concern",text:["A context view shows users and external systems. A container view shows deployable units and data stores. A component view shows major responsibilities inside one unit. Sequence diagrams show a specific runtime interaction, while deployment views show environments and infrastructure.","Each diagram needs scope, legend and a question it answers. Decorative boxes with arrows labelled 'data' do not communicate protocol, trust, direction or failure behavior."]},
      {title:"Boundaries need ownership and stable contracts",text:["Group code around cohesive capabilities such as identity, course catalog and progress, not solely technical folders. A module owns its rules and data access surface. Other modules call its public API instead of reaching through to its tables or internal files.","Dependency direction should point from volatile adapters toward stable domain contracts. This does not require an interface around every function. Create a seam where substitution, independent testing or ownership genuinely matters."]},
      {title:"Compatibility is designed over time",text:["For HTTP APIs, additive optional response fields are usually easier to tolerate than removing or changing meanings. Clients should ignore unknown fields where the contract permits. Validate requests strictly enough to catch mistakes without preventing documented evolution.","For events, name the event as a completed fact, include stable identity and version the schema deliberately. Consumers need idempotency because delivery may repeat. A schema registry or shared type package does not replace runtime compatibility and rollout sequencing."]},
      {title:"Distribution adds failure, not automatic scale",text:["A modular monolith can provide clear ownership, transactions and simple local operation. Split a service when independent scaling, deployment cadence, team ownership, regulatory isolation or failure containment produces measurable value that outweighs network, observability and data-consistency costs.","Do not create microservices as an advanced-course badge. First make boundaries explicit inside one process; a poorly bounded monolith becomes a poorly bounded distributed system with harder debugging."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"Modular monolith",steps:[{name:"HTTP adapter",detail:"external contract"},{name:"Identity module",detail:"principal and session"},{name:"Course module",detail:"catalog ownership"},{name:"Progress module",detail:"learning state"},{name:"Ports",detail:"explicit cross-module calls"},{name:"Adapters",detail:"database and integrations"}]},
    example:{title:"Contract evolution rule",language:"text",source:code(["Change: add optional `estimatedMinutes` to Module response","Server rollout: emits field only when known","Old client behavior: ignores unknown field","New client behavior: handles missing field with 'Not estimated'","Contract test: both representations accepted","Removal plan: none; field meaning documented and stable"]),notes:["Compatibility considers old and new participants simultaneously.","Optional does not mean semantically vague.","The contract test encodes both rollout states."]},
    practice:{title:"Redesign the learning service as owned modules",brief:"Create context, container, component and completion-sequence views, then define public contracts between identity, catalog and progress.",steps:["List external actors and systems.","Draw deployable containers and trust boundaries.","Assign each business invariant and table to one module owner.","Write public commands and queries between modules.","Draw the mark-complete sequence including failure and idempotency.","Evaluate one hypothetical service split using measurable drivers and added costs."],starter:code(["Module: Progress","Owns: completion records, prerequisite policy, progress summaries","Commands: completeModule(principal, learnerId, moduleId, revision)","Queries: resumeFor(principal, learnerId)","Events: ModuleCompleted v1","Must not expose: SQL tables or database client"]),criteria:["Every box has responsibility and owner","Arrows name calls, protocols or events","Trust boundaries and authorization points are visible","The sequence includes rejection and duplicate delivery","The service-split decision states evidence rather than fashion"],hints:["Start from business capabilities and invariants.","A diagram should make at least one design question easier to answer."]},
    diagnostic:{title:"Two modules update each other's tables",prompt:"What architectural property is missing?",options:["Color contrast","Data ownership and a public cross-module contract","A promise queue","A favicon"],answer:1,explanation:"Direct table access bypasses invariants and couples internal schemas across module boundaries."},
    checks:[{q:"What does a sequence diagram explain?",options:["Every source file","One runtime interaction over time","Only deployment regions","CSS inheritance"],answer:1,why:"It shows participants, messages, order and alternate outcomes for a scenario."},{q:"When should a service split be considered?",options:["Because advanced apps require it","When independent operational or organizational needs justify distribution cost","Whenever there are three modules","Before defining ownership"],answer:1,why:"Distribution is a tradeoff, not a maturity badge."},{q:"Who should access a module's tables?",options:["Any module","The owning module through its internal adapter","Every browser","Only tests"],answer:1,why:"Ownership protects invariants and enables controlled evolution."}],
    project:"Produce an architecture dossier for the platform with four purposeful views, module contracts, an API/event compatibility policy and a documented monolith-versus-services decision.",
    resources:["express","postgres","owasp-api"]
  });

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"amber", icon:"speed", title:"Performance and concurrency from evidence", minutes:165, prerequisites:["JS-024"],
    summary:"Performance work begins with a user or system objective and measured bottleneck. Learn latency distributions, profiling, event-loop health, algorithmic growth, caching, worker threads and load tests that do not turn one machine's anecdote into a universal claim.",
    outcomes:["Define measurable performance objectives","Profile CPU, memory, event-loop and database behavior","Apply caching with explicit correctness rules","Use worker threads only for justified CPU-bound work"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"Measure the experience, not one average",text:["Latency distributions reveal tail behavior hidden by a mean. State percentiles, window, traffic shape and endpoint. Throughput, concurrency, error rate and resource saturation belong beside latency. A service-level objective connects these measures to an acceptable user outcome.","Benchmark in a controlled environment with warmup, representative data and repeat runs. Compare before and after under the same conditions. One laptop result is local evidence, not a production capacity promise."]},
      {title:"Profile before changing architecture",text:["CPU profiles identify hot stacks; heap snapshots and allocation profiles explain retained memory; event-loop delay reveals blocking; database plans expose scans, joins and cardinality estimates. Correlate the profile with the slow request rather than optimizing code absent from the critical path.","Complexity analysis predicts growth, while profiling shows actual cost under one workload. Use both. A nested scan may be harmless for ten elements and catastrophic for a million."]},
      {title:"Caching creates consistency policy",text:["Define the cache key, value, maximum size, expiration, invalidation and behavior on dependency failure. Cache only data safe to share at that scope; including authorization-sensitive results under a broad key can leak information.","Use bounded caches. Time-to-live limits staleness but does not guarantee freshness. Invalidation on writes improves freshness but adds coupling. Measure hit rate, load reduction and stale behavior instead of assuming every cache helps."]},
      {title:"Parallelism belongs to CPU-bound isolation",text:["Node handles I/O concurrency through the event loop and platform facilities. Worker threads run JavaScript in parallel and suit substantial CPU tasks whose transfer and coordination cost is justified. Use a bounded pool, propagate cancellation and return structured results.","Spawning a worker per request is expensive and unbounded. For very large or independently scalable work, a job system or separate service may be more appropriate. Choose from workload, isolation and operational constraints."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"Performance investigation",steps:[{name:"Objective",detail:"user-visible threshold"},{name:"Representative load",detail:"traffic and data"},{name:"Measure",detail:"latency, rate, errors, saturation"},{name:"Profile",detail:"locate causal bottleneck"},{name:"Change one cause",detail:"smallest effective fix"},{name:"Compare",detail:"same conditions and regression gate"}]},
    example:{title:"Bounded in-memory cache",language:"javascript",source:code(["export class TtlCache {","  #entries = new Map();","  constructor({ maxEntries = 1000, now = Date.now } = {}) {","    this.maxEntries = maxEntries;","    this.now = now;","  }","  get(key) {","    const entry = this.#entries.get(key);","    if (!entry || entry.expiresAt <= this.now()) {","      this.#entries.delete(key);","      return undefined;","    }","    return entry.value;","  }","  set(key, value, ttlMs) {","    if (this.#entries.size >= this.maxEntries) {","      this.#entries.delete(this.#entries.keys().next().value);","    }","    this.#entries.set(key, { value, expiresAt: this.now() + ttlMs });","  }","}"]),notes:["Size and lifetime are bounded.","The clock is injectable for deterministic tests.","This simple eviction is insertion-order, not a complete LRU policy; the limitation is explicit."]},
    practice:{title:"Find and fix one real bottleneck",brief:"Load-test the progress summary, profile the slow path and make one evidence-led improvement.",steps:["Define latency, throughput and error objectives.","Create representative learner and completion data.","Run a controlled baseline with warmup and repeated samples.","Inspect Node CPU/event-loop evidence and the database query plan.","Change one identified cause—query/index, algorithm or bounded cache.","Repeat the same load and add a performance regression threshold with tolerance."],starter:code(["Scenario: 100 concurrent learners read progress","Dataset: 10,000 learners × 100 modules","Measure: p50 / p95 / p99 latency, errors, CPU, event-loop delay, DB time","Baseline:","Bottleneck evidence:","Change:","Comparable result:","Limitations:"]),criteria:["The workload and environment are recorded","The change targets measured evidence","Before/after conditions match","Correctness tests still pass","Claims remain local to the measured setup"],hints:["Start with the query plan and event-loop delay, not a cache.","Do not optimize a mocked database and call it database performance."]},
    diagnostic:{title:"Average improved, users still report slowness",prompt:"Which missing measure could reveal occasional long requests?",options:["A font weight","Tail percentiles such as p95 and p99","File extension","Module export count"],answer:1,explanation:"An average can hide a small but important set of very slow requests."},
    checks:[{q:"What comes before optimization?",options:["A measured objective and bottleneck","A worker per request","A global cache","A framework rewrite"],answer:0,why:"Without evidence, the change may miss the limiting path or harm correctness."},{q:"What must a cache define?",options:["Only a variable name","Key, scope, bound, freshness and invalidation behavior","A browser theme","A Git branch"],answer:1,why:"Caching is a consistency and resource policy."},{q:"When are worker threads useful?",options:["For every fetch","For substantial CPU-bound JavaScript with bounded coordination","To fix SQL injection","To render CSS"],answer:1,why:"Workers provide parallel JavaScript execution at a coordination cost."}],
    project:"Create a reproducible performance report for one critical journey with workload, distributions, profiles, one verified improvement, correctness reruns and explicit limitations.",
    resources:["node-api","vscode-debug","postgres"]
  });

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"green", icon:"pulse", title:"Reliability, observability and graceful operation", minutes:170, prerequisites:["JS-025"],
    summary:"Reliable systems make failure bounded, visible and recoverable. Learn structured telemetry, request correlation, health and readiness, graceful shutdown, dependency budgets, circuit breaking, background work and failure injection.",
    outcomes:["Instrument critical journeys with logs, metrics and traces","Design startup, readiness and shutdown states","Budget time and concurrency across dependencies","Exercise failure instead of inferring resilience from happy paths"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"Telemetry answers different questions",text:["Structured logs describe discrete events with stable fields. Metrics summarize rates, durations and gauges across time. Traces connect work across components and show where latency accumulated. One signal does not replace the others.","Attach a correlation or trace identifier at the request boundary and propagate context. Avoid high-cardinality metric labels such as user ID. Redact secrets and sensitive bodies before telemetry leaves the process."]},
      {title:"Health and readiness are different",text:["Liveness asks whether the process should be restarted. Readiness asks whether it should receive traffic. During startup, required dependencies initialize before readiness becomes true. During shutdown, readiness turns false before connections drain.","A health endpoint that performs expensive dependency work on every probe can create load or cascade failure. Define which checks belong in which signal and how operators should act on them."]},
      {title:"Resilience consumes a finite budget",text:["Timeouts, retries, bulkheads and circuit breakers bound failure propagation. Allocate a caller's total deadline across queueing, attempts and dependencies. A retry increases load on an already failing system, so use it only for safe transient cases and include jitter and caps.","A circuit breaker can fail fast after repeated dependency failure and later probe recovery. It adds state and tuning complexity; use it when measured failure behavior justifies it. Concurrency limits often provide a simpler first defense."]},
      {title:"Graceful shutdown is an executable lifecycle",text:["On SIGTERM, mark unready, stop accepting new requests, abort or finish owned background work, close servers and pools within a deadline, flush essential telemetry and exit with an appropriate code. Make the sequence idempotent because multiple triggers can occur.","Inject dependency latency, connection failure, malformed responses, process signals and partial background-job failure. Verify both user behavior and telemetry. A unit test named 'reliable' is not evidence of actual lifecycle behavior."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"Service lifecycle",steps:[{name:"Starting",detail:"load validated config"},{name:"Initializing",detail:"connect required dependencies"},{name:"Ready",detail:"accept bounded work"},{name:"Degraded",detail:"surface dependency state"},{name:"Draining",detail:"unready and stop new work"},{name:"Stopped",detail:"resources released or deadline recorded"}]},
    example:{title:"Idempotent shutdown coordinator",language:"javascript",source:code(["export function createShutdown({ server, pool, background, deadlineMs = 10000 }) {","  let running;","  return function shutdown(reason) {","    if (running) return running;","    running = (async () => {","      readiness.set(false);","      const deadline = AbortSignal.timeout(deadlineMs);","      await Promise.allSettled([","        closeServer(server, deadline),","        background.stop(deadline),","        pool.end()","      ]);","      logger.info({ reason }, \"shutdown complete\");","    })();","    return running;","  };","}"]),notes:["Repeated signals share one shutdown promise.","Readiness changes before draining.","All owned resources receive a bounded close attempt; outcomes still need inspection and exit policy."]},
    practice:{title:"Instrument and break the resume journey",brief:"Add correlated telemetry to the resume endpoint, then inject slow and failed database behavior and prove the service responds within its budget.",steps:["Define a trace span and low-cardinality metrics for the journey.","Add structured safe logs at boundary and failure decisions.","Propagate the request deadline to the repository.","Inject database latency beyond the budget.","Verify the client receives a safe bounded failure and the telemetry identifies the database span.","Send SIGTERM during in-flight work and verify readiness/draining behavior."],starter:code(["Signal | Required fields | Question answered","Trace | requestId, route, db span | where was time spent?","Metric | route template, status class | how often and how long?","Log | requestId, safe error category | what decision failed?","Lifecycle | state, reason, duration | did shutdown drain?" ]),criteria:["No secret or high-cardinality user value becomes a metric label","A slow dependency cannot exceed the total request budget silently","Readiness is false during drain","Shutdown is safe when triggered twice","Failure injection produces expected user and telemetry evidence"],hints:["Use route templates such as `/learners/:id`, not raw URLs, in metric labels.","A timeout without cancelling the query can leave hidden work." ]},
    diagnostic:{title:"Metrics system became expensive",prompt:"Every metric label included learnerId. What design problem occurred?",options:["High-cardinality dimensions created unbounded series","The event loop stopped parsing","A transaction rolled back","The browser cached HTML"],answer:0,explanation:"Per-user labels create many unique time series; use traces or logs for request-specific detail."},
    checks:[{q:"What is readiness for?",options:["Proving all business logic","Signaling whether the instance should receive traffic","Choosing a font","Replacing tests"],answer:1,why:"Traffic routing should stop before or during unsafe lifecycle states."},{q:"Why propagate one deadline?",options:["To let every layer spend a full independent timeout","To keep total request work within the caller's budget","To disable cancellation","To increase retries"],answer:1,why:"Independent full timeouts can multiply end-to-end latency."},{q:"What validates graceful shutdown?",options:["Reading the handler","Executing signal and in-flight scenarios with observed resource behavior","A build","A lint rule"],answer:1,why:"Lifecycle claims require direct execution."}],
    project:"Deliver an operational evidence pack with journey traces, meaningful metrics, safe logs, readiness/liveness contracts, failure injection and executed graceful-shutdown scenarios.",
    resources:["otel","node-api","owasp-node"]
  });

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"orange", icon:"deploy", title:"CI, containers, migrations and deployment evidence", minutes:175, prerequisites:["JS-026"],
    summary:"Delivery should promote the same reviewed artifact through explicit gates. Learn reproducible installs, CI stages, container boundaries, secrets, schema migration sequencing, smoke checks, rollback and the difference between local packaging and deployed verification.",
    outcomes:["Design a reproducible CI pipeline","Build a least-privilege container artifact","Sequence code and database changes compatibly","Define deployment, smoke and rollback evidence"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"Continuous integration is a trustworthy gate",text:["A clean runner installs from the lockfile, checks types and syntax, runs tests, builds once and retains the artifact and evidence. Fail fast on deterministic checks but preserve enough output for diagnosis. Pin action versions deliberately and minimize workflow permissions.","Separate checks can run concurrently when independent, but publishing must depend on all required gates. Cache only derived dependencies with keys tied to the lockfile; never allow a stale cache to override the locked graph."]},
      {title:"A container is a process boundary, not a security guarantee",text:["Use a small supported runtime base, copy only runtime files, run as a non-root user, define the working directory and handle signals in the Node process. Multi-stage builds can keep compilers and development dependencies out of the runtime image.","Do not bake secrets into layers or client assets. Supply runtime configuration through the deployment environment. Scan and update base images, but verify application behavior after updates."]},
      {title:"Schema change and code rollout are one compatibility plan",text:["Use expand-and-contract: add compatible schema, deploy code that tolerates old and new representations, backfill in bounded batches, verify, enforce new constraints, switch reads and remove old structures in a later release. Write down which steps are reversible.","A failed code deployment can often roll back to a prior artifact; a completed destructive data migration may not. Backups and restore rehearsals support recovery but do not remove the need for compatible rollout design."]},
      {title:"Deployment is proven in the target environment",text:["A deployment record identifies artifact digest, configuration version, migration status and environment. Smoke tests exercise critical paths against the deployed endpoint. Observe health, errors and latency through a defined window, then promote or roll back according to thresholds.","A local container build is implementation evidence. A CI success is pipeline evidence. Neither proves the artifact is deployed, reachable, configured correctly or verified on a named device. Report each stage separately."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"Artifact promotion",steps:[{name:"Commit",detail:"reviewed source and lockfile"},{name:"CI",detail:"clean checks and tests"},{name:"Build once",detail:"immutable artifact digest"},{name:"Migrate",detail:"compatible schema stage"},{name:"Deploy",detail:"environment configuration"},{name:"Verify",detail:"smoke, telemetry and rollback decision"}]},
    example:{title:"CI gate outline",language:"yaml",source:code(["permissions:","  contents: read","jobs:","  verify:","    steps:","      - uses: actions/checkout@<pinned-commit>","      - uses: actions/setup-node@<pinned-commit>","        with:","          node-version-file: .node-version","          cache: npm","      - run: npm ci","      - run: npm run check","      - run: npm test","      - run: npm run build"]),notes:["Pin third-party actions to reviewed immutable revisions in a real workflow.","The lockfile controls dependency resolution.","Deployment would be a separate authorized job with environment controls."]},
    practice:{title:"Write an executable release plan",brief:"Create a local and CI delivery workflow for the platform, including a compatible schema change and rollback decision points.",steps:["Pin the supported Node line and clean install command.","Define check, unit, integration, browser and build gates.","Create a non-root multi-stage container.","Plan an additive progress-note migration with backfill and enforcement.","Define artifact identity, deployed smoke checks and telemetry thresholds.","Rehearse local migration forward and application rollback against the expanded schema."],starter:code(["Stage | Input | Gate | Evidence | Rollback","Verify | commit + lockfile | all local suites | reports | fix forward","Build | verified source | image scan/smoke | digest | discard artifact","Expand | additive migration | schema check | migration row | migration-specific","Deploy | same digest | readiness + journeys | deployment record | prior digest","Contract | verified adoption | old use = zero | query evidence | planned restore"]),criteria:["One immutable artifact is promoted","Secrets are absent from image and client bundle","The old application survives the expanded schema","Smoke tests exercise critical deployed journeys in the intended design","Rollback limits are explicit","No deployment claim is made from local rehearsal"],hints:["Separate database compatibility from container rollback.","Record the artifact digest at every environment boundary."]},
    diagnostic:{title:"Rollback failed after dropping a column",prompt:"Why could redeploying the old container not recover?",options:["Code rollback does not reverse destructive data/schema changes","Containers cannot run JavaScript","Git lost the commit","The health endpoint used JSON"],answer:0,explanation:"Application artifacts and persistent data evolve independently; destructive migrations require their own recovery plan."},
    checks:[{q:"Why use `npm ci` in CI?",options:["To ignore the lockfile","To perform a clean install that honors the lockfile","To publish automatically","To disable scripts"],answer:1,why:"It provides a strict clean install for the recorded graph."},{q:"Where do runtime secrets belong?",options:["Client Vite variables","Container image layers","A controlled deployment secret mechanism","The Git README"],answer:2,why:"Secrets must not be embedded in source, artifacts or public bundles."},{q:"What proves deployment?",options:["A local build","A verified target-environment deployment record and exercised checks","TypeScript compilation","A Dockerfile"],answer:1,why:"Deployment is an external state requiring direct evidence."}],
    project:"Produce a complete delivery design with clean CI, immutable artifact, non-root container, compatible migration rehearsal, target smoke plan and an evidence ledger that separates every release stage.",
    resources:["github-actions","docker","node-releases","owasp-node"]
  });

  M({
    track:"Advanced", accent:"pink", icon:"capstone", title:"Advanced capstone: a production-shaped learning platform", minutes:720, prerequisites:["JS-027"],
    summary:"Build a coherent TypeScript, React, Node.js and PostgreSQL platform that demonstrates depth through complete journeys and direct evidence. The capstone integrates architecture, security, performance, reliability and delivery without pretending that local work is production deployment.",
    outcomes:["Integrate typed frontend, API and database contracts","Demonstrate security and reliability through negative and failure tests","Measure one performance objective and causal improvement","Package a reviewable evidence dossier"],
    chapters:[
      {title:"Product scope is intentionally narrow",text:["Complete four journeys: learner resumes a course, completes a module, reviews progress and an instructor publishes a lesson revision. Support session expiry and concurrent revision conflict. Do not add billing, social feeds, real-time chat or generative AI; unfinished breadth weakens the evidence.","The optional future Ministral 3 14B companion remains outside this build. Its eventual flow is browser to Babak's backend, then n8n/Postgres to the model and back through a validated response. The frontend will never hold model credentials or call the model directly, and core learning remains independent."]},
      {title:"Architecture and data preserve ownership",text:["Use a Vite React TypeScript client, an Express TypeScript modular monolith and PostgreSQL. Identity, catalog and progress modules own their contracts and tables. Runtime validation protects every external boundary. Session cookies and object policy enforce server authority.","Document context, container, component, data and sequence views. Include trust boundaries, error outcomes and lifecycle—not only the happy path."]},
      {title:"Quality is exercised under stress and failure",text:["Layer unit, integration, contract and browser tests. Use real PostgreSQL for data behavior and real HTTP for serialization. Test cross-user access, expired sessions, malformed bodies, duplicate completion, stale revisions, dependency timeout, graceful shutdown and browser cancellation.","Define one performance objective for resume or progress, run a representative baseline, profile, improve one cause and rerun correctness. Add correlated telemetry and prove that the injected failure is visible without leaking secrets."]},
      {title:"The dossier states exactly what is known",text:["The repository includes setup, platform differences, architecture decisions, threat model, API contract, migrations, operational runbook and evidence ledger. CI builds one artifact; local rehearsal proves the pipeline steps that actually ran.","Mark implemented, locally verified, independently reviewed, deployed and target-device verified separately. If no external deployment was authorized, say so. Precision is part of engineering quality."]}
    ],
    model:{title:"Capstone system",steps:[{name:"React client",detail:"accessible typed journeys"},{name:"HTTP boundary",detail:"runtime validation and session"},{name:"Modular services",detail:"policy and domain rules"},{name:"PostgreSQL",detail:"owned schemas and constraints"},{name:"Telemetry",detail:"correlated evidence"},{name:"Delivery",detail:"immutable artifact and staged claims"}]},
    example:{title:"Capstone definition of done",language:"text",source:code(["Journey evidence: 4 browser paths + API contracts + DB invariants","Security evidence: threat model + cross-user/expiry/input negative tests","Reliability evidence: timeout/cancellation/shutdown/failure injection","Performance evidence: objective + workload + profile + before/after","Delivery evidence: clean CI + artifact digest + migration rehearsal","Status evidence: local/review/deploy/device reported separately"]),notes:["Each row names a different claim and evidence source.","A high test count cannot substitute for missing security or lifecycle scenarios.","Unperformed deployment remains a clear limitation, not a footnote."]},
    practice:{title:"Execute by vertical journey and quality attribute",brief:"Create the ledger and diagrams first, then deliver each user journey with its security, failure and evidence obligations before moving on.",steps:["Freeze the bounded scope and acceptance examples.","Create data migrations and module contracts.","Deliver resume, completion, progress and publish-revision slices.","Run the negative security matrix.","Run failure injection and lifecycle scenarios.","Measure and improve one bottleneck.","Build and rehearse the release path.","Perform a separate adversarial pass against every ledger row."],starter:code(["Requirement | Implementation | Direct evidence | Status","Resume learning | | browser + API + DB |","Cross-user denial | | authenticated negative integration |","Session expiry | | browser/API lifecycle |","Graceful shutdown | | signal + in-flight execution |","Performance objective | | repeatable load/profile |","Deployment | | target environment record | NOT AUTHORIZED"]),criteria:["All four journeys are complete and accessible","External inputs are runtime-validated","Cross-user and session-lifecycle behavior is directly tested","Failure, shutdown and observability paths are executed","Performance claim has controlled evidence","The evidence dossier contains no collapsed or unsupported status"],hints:["A vertical slice includes its negative and failure states, not only success.","Use the ledger to decide what remains—not to decorate the final report." ]},
    diagnostic:{title:"Hundreds of unit tests passed",prompt:"Can that alone establish accessible browser behavior, real PostgreSQL constraints, graceful shutdown and deployment?",options:["Yes, test count is enough","No; each claim needs evidence at its actual boundary","Yes, if coverage is 100%","Only if the code is TypeScript"],answer:1,explanation:"Tests prove only the behavior and environment they directly exercise."},
    checks:[{q:"What makes this capstone advanced?",options:["The number of frameworks","Completed journeys under explicit security, data, failure, performance and delivery constraints","A long README","Microservices"],answer:1,why:"Depth comes from reasoning and direct evidence across constraints."},{q:"Where may the future model credential exist?",options:["React bundle","Browser localStorage","Only in the controlled backend path","A query string"],answer:2,why:"The browser never receives direct model credentials."},{q:"What should the report say without a deployment?",options:["Production ready and verified","Locally implemented and verified within the stated scope; deployment unverified","Deployed by implication","Nothing"],answer:1,why:"Evidence stages must remain distinct."}],
    project:"Deliver the complete production-shaped platform and evidence dossier, leaving deployment and target-device status accurate to what was actually authorized and exercised.",
    resources:["typescript","react","vite","express","postgres","owasp-auth","owasp-api","otel","docker","github-actions"]
  });
  [
    ["JS-022",[
      "Use `Object.getPrototypeOf`, `Object.hasOwn` and property descriptors to investigate actual behavior instead of trusting a diagram. Avoid the legacy `__proto__` accessor in application code; explicit standard operations communicate intent and handle edge cases more predictably.",
      "Private fields are branded to instances created by the declaring class, which makes borrowed methods fail rather than exposing private state. Composition can keep state in closures or focused collaborators when class identity is not part of the domain.",
      "An iterator is stateful and generally consumed once, while an iterable can create a fresh iterator for each traversal. State that distinction in an API because returning the same iterator can make a second loop appear mysteriously empty.",
      "Proxy traps should forward ordinary behavior with Reflect and preserve non-configurable-property invariants. Measure hot-path impact and test identity-sensitive libraries before adopting a proxy for validation, logging or reactive behavior."
    ]],
    ["JS-023",[
      "Read generated declarations and JavaScript when configuration is uncertain. The checker can model a dependency differently from the code Node loads, so execute an emitted production entry point in addition to running `tsc --noEmit`.",
      "Prefer a small union of meaningful states over booleans such as `isLoading`, `hasError` and `hasData`. The union forces every render or service decision to acknowledge which combinations can actually exist.",
      "A constraint such as `T extends { id: string }` allows only operations guaranteed by that shape while preserving additional caller information. If the implementation returns an unrelated asserted type, the generic promise is dishonest.",
      "Keep one reviewed `tsconfig` base and explain project-specific overrides. Include type checking, emitted-module execution and runtime boundary tests in migration evidence; no single one of these verifies the other two."
    ]],
    ["JS-024",[
      "Rendering may be interrupted or repeated, so reading time, generating random identity or mutating a module-level collection during render creates unstable output. Move event-caused work to handlers and external synchronization to effects with cleanup.",
      "Choose state identity deliberately. Updating a nested object requires copying every changed path, while normalization can make entity updates clearer. Do not memoize by habit; first measure whether recomputation or rerendering is actually significant.",
      "An effect should be safe across setup, cleanup and immediate setup again. Capture the request identity or AbortController created by that effect, and ensure cleanup only cancels the resources that particular execution owns.",
      "Test the production base path and direct navigation used by the host, not only the root dev server. Inspect the built bundle for accidental environment values and verify keyboard and error journeys in a real browser."
    ]],
    ["JS-025",[
      "Add assumptions beside each threat: whether the browser is trusted, where TLS terminates, who operates the database and which identities can reach administrative paths. A threat model without system boundaries cannot prioritize controls honestly.",
      "Protect account recovery as strongly as sign-in because it can replace credentials. Rotate session identity after authentication or privilege elevation, expire it server-side and test that an old session cannot continue after logout.",
      "Evaluate authorization after loading enough trusted target context, and repeat it for reads, writes and bulk operations. Add a matrix of principals versus actions so a new role does not inherit access through an untested default.",
      "Treat logs, backups, analytics and support exports as additional data paths. Redaction at the HTTP response is insufficient if credentials or private records remain in telemetry, crash dumps or retained test fixtures."
    ]],
    ["JS-026",[
      "Put a title, scope, date and legend on every view, then review it against source and deployment configuration. A stale diagram can be more harmful than no diagram because reviewers may approve controls at boundaries that no longer exist.",
      "Enforce module direction with import rules or focused architecture tests where drift is likely. Public contracts should expose domain operations, not generic repositories that let every caller reconstruct rules differently.",
      "Write compatibility examples for old client/new server, new client/old server and repeated event delivery. A field can be syntactically optional yet operationally required by one client, so rollout evidence must include actual consumers.",
      "Before distributing, quantify traffic, deployment conflict, failure isolation and team ownership. Also budget service discovery, authentication between services, tracing, retries, data ownership and incident response; these costs arrive immediately even when scale does not."
    ]],
    ["JS-027",[
      "Tie the objective to a journey and population, for example p95 resume latency under a named concurrency and dataset. Report confidence and run-to-run variance so a small fluctuation is not presented as an architectural improvement.",
      "Capture a baseline profile before editing, annotate the suspected stack and rerun the identical scenario after one change. If the bottleneck moves, profile again; the next largest stack is not automatically worth optimizing.",
      "Prevent a cache stampede by coordinating concurrent misses when the workload requires it. Include tenant, authorization and representation inputs in the key, or cache only public data whose sharing policy is explicit.",
      "Use transferable data or shared memory only with a clear synchronization design. Worker exceptions, exits, queue saturation and cancellation are part of the caller contract, not background implementation details."
    ]],
    ["JS-028",[
      "Choose telemetry from an operational question and retention policy. A request count needs stable route and status dimensions; a trace can carry high-cardinality identifiers; a log records a decision with redacted context and severity.",
      "Keep liveness shallow enough to avoid restart storms. Readiness may reflect required dependency or migration state, but define degraded operation explicitly so one optional integration does not unnecessarily remove every instance from service.",
      "Calculate the retry budget inside the original deadline and expose retry counts in telemetry. Combine concurrency limits with queue bounds so callers receive controlled overload rather than waiting until memory or connection pools collapse.",
      "During shutdown, reject new work before draining, wait only to the deadline and record unfinished ownership. Exercise the sequence in the actual server process because test doubles cannot prove sockets and database pools close correctly."
    ]],
    ["JS-029",[
      "Run CI from a clean checkout with no developer-global tools or untracked files. Preserve machine-readable reports and the exact runtime versions so a failure can be reproduced rather than dismissed as runner-specific behavior.",
      "Set a read-only filesystem where possible, drop Linux capabilities, expose only the needed port and define writable temporary locations explicitly. Container boundaries reduce accidental access but do not replace application authorization or host hardening.",
      "Measure backfill rate, lock duration and replication impact before production execution. Pause safely between batches, make progress resumable and verify old and new code against the expanded schema before adding restrictive constraints.",
      "A smoke check should cross the deployed routing, process and required dependencies using safe data. Pair it with telemetry thresholds and an accountable rollback decision; a health endpoint alone can pass while the primary journey fails."
    ]],
    ["JS-030",[
      "Write explicit non-goals beside the four journeys and reject additions that do not strengthen their evidence. Advanced work means completing hard operational qualities around a coherent product, not maximizing the number of screens or packages.",
      "Assign each invariant to one module and each table to one owner. Contract tests verify cross-boundary representations, while database integration tests verify constraints; TypeScript types alone cannot establish either behavior. Document every ownership decision in the architecture dossier.",
      "Run the security matrix with distinct authenticated users and the lifecycle suite in a spawned process. Store profiles, traces and test reports with environment details so reviewers can distinguish executed evidence from design intention.",
      "End with a claim ledger that links every acceptance statement to an artifact or executed result. Mark deployment and target-device rows unverified until those exact environments are exercised, even when local browser evidence is excellent."
    ]]
  ].forEach(([id, paragraphs]) => C.addDeepDive(id, paragraphs));
})();
